{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-40863","model_call":18,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1054"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1054"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_25_1463836094#8_2825251705","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1054","rank":3,"snippet":"often positive than serum-specific IgE and correlate better with history. Baseline tryptase should be measured. Those with raised levels have a higher risk of severe systemic reactions. Treatment of patients with bee or wasp sting allergy All patients with a history of systemic reaction should be immediately provided with a written emergency management plan, an adrenaline (epinephrine) auto-injector and education in its use. With children, appropriate liaison with the school is recommended. Venom immunotherapy (VIT) is recommended for all patients with a severe systemic reaction after a sting. It reduces the chances of a serious <cover>allergic reaction to an insect sting and improves quality of life. </cover>VIT is the only specific treatment that is currently available for patients with a history of systemic reaction to a Hymenoptera insect sting. VIT is effective in 95% of patients allergic to wasp venom and about 80% of those allergic to bee venom. VIT is not often recommended for children.\" , \"start_char\": 4702.000000 , \"title\": \"Insect Bites and Stings Treatment Information | Patient\" , \"url\": \"https://patient.info/doctor/insect-bites-and-stings-pro\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_25_1463836094#8_2825251705","rank":3,"relevance":3}]},"time":15},"model_calls":18,"document_judgments":13,"relevance_3":7,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"request","topic_id":"2024-40863","model_call":18,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1054"},"model_calls":18,"document_judgments":13,"relevance_3":7,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-40863","model_call":18,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1054"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1054"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_25_1463836094#9_2825253470","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1054","rank":4,"snippet":"This article is for Medical Professionals Hymenoptera stings Systemic reaction to wasp or bee stings Investigation of patients with bee or wasp sting allergy Treatment of patients with bee or wasp sting allergy Management of bee or wasp stings Blood-sucking flies True bugs (Hemiptera) Ticks (Ixodoidea) Management Harvest mites (Trombiculidae) Join our weekly wellness digest from the best health experts in the business \" , \"segment\": \"Venom immunotherapy (VIT) is recommended for all patients with a severe systemic reaction after a sting. It reduces the chances of a serious <cover>allergic reaction to an insect sting and improves quality of life. </cover>VIT is the only specific treatment that is currently available for patients with a history of systemic reaction to a Hymenoptera insect sting. VIT is effective in 95% of patients allergic to wasp venom and about 80% of those allergic to bee venom. VIT is not often recommended for children. A Cochrane review found that approximately 1 in 10 people treated had an allergic reaction during their treatment. The usual duration of VIT is three years in the UK. All patients should be advised of measures to reduce their risk of future stings. These include: ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_25_1463836094#9_2825253470","rank":4,"relevance":3}]},"time":6},"model_calls":18,"document_judgments":13,"relevance_3":7,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"request","topic_id":"2024-40863","model_call":18,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1054"},"model_calls":18,"document_judgments":13,"relevance_3":7,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-40863","model_call":18,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1054"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1054"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_05_1004088852#13_1823916429","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1054","rank":5,"snippet":"and wasp stings? What is the treatment for bee and wasp stings? Prevention Treatment of localised site reactions Treatment for systemic reactions Venom immunotherapy desensitisation What is the outcome for bee and wasp stings? Related information Bibliography On DermNet NZ Other websites Skin cancer Dermatology Made Easy Book Call for sponsors Sign up to the newsletter \" , \"segment\": \"Protection usually lasts 1–3 years after discontinuation. Insect venoms are commercially available for honey bee, paper wasp, and yellow jacket European <cover>wasp. A Cochrane review reported venom immunotherapy significantly reduces the risk of systemic and large local reactions, and is likely to reduce the risk of anaphylaxis, improving quality of life. </cover>Venom immunotherapy can be recommended for: Children with systemic sting reactions not limited to the skin Adults with systemic reactions including those limited to the skin High risk factors including beekeepers, patients with clonal mast cell disorders Impaired quality of life following sting reactions NOT recommended for toxic reactions or those sensitised but with no clinical symptoms. What is the outcome for bee and wasp stings? The majority of bee and wasp sting reactions are localised and resolve in hours to days. Episodes are usually ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_05_1004088852#13_1823916429","rank":5,"relevance":3}]},"time":9},"model_calls":18,"document_judgments":13,"relevance_3":7,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
