{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-40863","model_call":31,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1065"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1065"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_56_1391180600#11_2762246097","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1065","rank":3,"snippet":"sting) as a result of a sting, there is no need to perform any venom allergy testing. 3 ﻿ Or, if a child or adult has a large local reaction, where swelling occurs at the site of the sting only, it is not usually a reason to perform venom testing or to administer venom allergy shots. This is because the chance of developing anaphylaxis with future stings is only about 0% to 7% for both children and adults. 6 ﻿ A few studies show that these <cover>large local reactions can be decreased with the use of venom immunotherapy. This may be required in situations where stings are frequent and the swelling disrupts a person’s quality of life </cover>or ability to work. 3 ﻿ Testing is needed if a person of any age has symptoms of anaphylaxis after being stung. That's because the person has about a 30% to 60% chance that future insect stings will cause a similar reaction. 3 ﻿ Also, if there is a particular parental concern or the child is at high risk for frequent stings, venom testing and treatment is a reasonable option. People older than 16 with these same concerns should have venom testing and treatment, given ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_56_1391180600#11_2762246097","rank":3,"relevance":3}]},"time":8},"model_calls":31,"document_judgments":22,"relevance_3":8,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
