{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-40863","model_call":40,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"swelling disrupts\" \"quality of life\" \"bee\" \"sting\")"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"swelling disrupts\" \"quality of life\" \"bee\" \"sting\")"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_56_1391180600#11_2762246097","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1074","rank":1,"snippet":"to administer venom allergy shots. This is because the chance of developing anaphylaxis with future stings is only about 0% to 7% for both children and adults. 6 ﻿ A few studies show that these large local reactions can be decreased with the use of venom immunotherapy. This may be required in situations where stings are frequent and the <cover>swelling disrupts a person’s quality of life or ability to work. 3 ﻿ Testing is needed if a person of any age has symptoms of anaphylaxis after being stung. That's because the person has about a 30% to 60% chance that future insect stings will cause a similar reaction. 3 ﻿ Also, if there is a particular parental concern or the child is at high risk for frequent stings, venom testing and treatment is a reasonable option. People older than 16 with these same concerns should have venom testing and treatment, given a higher risk of anaphylaxis with future stings.\" , \"start_char\": 7926.000000 , \"title\": \"Overview of Bee Sting </cover>Reactions and Allergies\" , \"url\": \"https://www.verywell.com/bee-sting-allergic-reactions-83016\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_56_1391180600#11_2762246097","rank":1,"relevance":3}]},"time":68},"model_calls":40,"document_judgments":26,"relevance_3":8,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
