{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-40863","model_call":44,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1077"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1077"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_32_853332099#12_1643577752","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1077","rank":4,"snippet":"dust mites. Skin testing may help diagnose food allergies as well. Allergy skin tests are widely used to help diagnose allergic conditions, including: Hay fever (allergic rhinitis) Allergic asthma Dermatitis (eczema) Food allergies Penicillin allergy<cover>Bee venom allergy Latex allergy Why see an allergist? Who is an Allergist/Immunologist? An allergist/immunologist (commonly referred to as an allergist) is a physician specially trained to diagnose, treat and manage allergies, asthma and immunologic disorders including primary immunodeficiency disorders. These conditions range from the very common to the very rare, spanning all ages and encompassing various organ systems. In the United States, becoming an allergist / immunologist requires at least an additional nine years of training beyond a bachelor’s degree. When should you see an allergist? If you feel like you’re always getting sick, with a cough or head congestion, it’s time to see an allergist. Often, the symptoms of asthma or allergies develop gradually over time and as a result, your quality of life </cover>becomes poorer. Allergy sufferers may become used to frequent symptoms such as sneezing, nasal congestion or wheezing. With the help of an allergist, these symptoms usually can be prevented or controlled with major improvement in ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_32_853332099#12_1643577752","rank":4,"relevance":1}]},"time":6},"model_calls":44,"document_judgments":28,"relevance_3":8,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"request","topic_id":"2024-40863","model_call":44,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1077"},"model_calls":44,"document_judgments":28,"relevance_3":8,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-40863","model_call":44,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1077"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1077"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_05_1004088852#8_1823903949","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1077","rank":5,"snippet":"bee and wasp stings? Related information Bibliography On DermNet NZ Other websites Skin cancer Dermatology Made Easy Book Call for sponsors Sign up to the newsletter \" , \"segment\": \"Blistering Secondary bacterial skin infection, including cellulitis and lymphangitis Persistent reaction lasting weeks or months Vasculitis, serum sickness, neurological effects Impact on <cover>quality of life — anxiety resulting in social restriction. Unusual reactions to bee and wasp stings Wasp sting reaction persisted for more than 6 weeks Wasp sting reaction causing localised vasculitis with purpura Blistering reaction to a bee sting How is bee and wasp sting allergy diagnosed? Both skin testing and serologic tests should be performed on patients with a history of a systemic reaction to a bee or wasp sting, and considered for those with large local reactions. Allergy skin testing: skin prick testing and intradermal testing using purified venom </cover>at least two weeks after a sting reaction. Serology: serum IgE testing for antibodies against specific insect venoms. Basophil activation test: useful if the skin test is negative but the sIgE is positive. Baseline serum tryptase:\" , \"start_char\": 4032.000000 , \"title\": \"Bee and ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_05_1004088852#8_1823903949","rank":5,"relevance":3}]},"time":7},"model_calls":44,"document_judgments":28,"relevance_3":8,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
