{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-40863","model_call":46,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"bee sting\" (+ anxiety fear) (+ \"social restriction\" \"quality of life\" work daily))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"bee sting\" (+ anxiety fear) (+ \"social restriction\" \"quality of life\" work daily))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_05_1004088852#7_1823900839","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1078","rank":1,"snippet":"see Allergies explained] Onset 10 minutes after sting Urticaria, angioedema, itch Vomiting and diarrhoea Anaphylaxis Bronchoconstriction Abdominal cramping and vomiting Cardiorespiratory arrest within 5–10 minutes Systemic toxic reactions (envenomation) Usually follows 500 or more stings Dizziness, nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea Cardiac, liver, renal, muscle damage Can be fatal What are the complications of bee and wasp stings? Blistering Secondary bacterial skin infection, including cellulitis and lymphangitis Persistent reaction lasting weeks or months Vasculitis, serum sickness, neurological effects Impact on quality of life — <cover>anxiety resulting in social restriction. Unusual reactions to bee and wasp stings Wasp sting reaction persisted for more than 6 weeks Wasp sting reaction causing localised vasculitis with purpura Blistering reaction to a bee sting</cover>How is bee and wasp sting allergy diagnosed? Both skin testing and serologic tests should be performed on patients with a history of a systemic reaction to a bee or wasp sting, and considered for those with large local reactions. Allergy skin testing: skin prick testing and intradermal testing using purified venom at least two weeks after a sting reaction.\" , \"start_char\": 2946.000000 , \"title\": \"Bee and wasp stings | DermNet NZ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_05_1004088852#7_1823900839","rank":1,"relevance":3}]},"time":2332},"model_calls":46,"document_judgments":30,"relevance_3":8,"max_doc