{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-40863","model_call":53,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1085"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1085"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_05_1004088852#13_1823916429","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1085","rank":5,"snippet":"current knowledge on bee venom and bee envenoming therapy. Front Immunol. 2019;10:2090. What are the clinical features of bee and wasp stings? What are the complications of bee and wasp stings? How is bee and <cover>wasp sting allergy diagnosed? What is the differential diagnosis for bee and wasp stings? What is the treatment for bee and wasp stings? Prevention Treatment of localised site reactions Treatment for systemic reactions Venom immunotherapy desensitisation What is the outcome for bee and wasp stings? Related information Bibliography On DermNet NZ Other websites Skin cancer Dermatology Made Easy Book Call for sponsors Sign up to the newsletter \" , \"segment\": \"Protection usually lasts 1–3 years after discontinuation. Insect venoms are commercially available for honey bee, paper wasp, and yellow jacket European wasp. A Cochrane review reported venom immunotherapy significantly reduces the risk of systemic and large local reactions, and is likely to reduce the risk of anaphylaxis, improving quality of life. </cover>Venom immunotherapy can be recommended for: Children with systemic sting reactions not limited to the skin Adults with systemic reactions including those limited to the skin High risk factors including beekeepers, patients with clonal mast cell disorders","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_05_1004088852#13_1823916429","rank":5,"relevance":3}]},"time":7},"model_calls":53,"document_judgments":31,"relevance_3":8,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
