{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-40863","model_call":53,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1085"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1085"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_05_1004088852#12_1823913740","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1085","rank":7,"snippet":"<cover>wasp sting allergy diagnosed? What is the differential diagnosis for bee and wasp stings? What is the treatment for bee and wasp stings? Prevention Treatment of localised site reactions Treatment for systemic reactions Venom immunotherapy desensitisation What is the outcome for bee and wasp stings? Related information Bibliography On DermNet NZ Other websites Skin cancer Dermatology Made Easy Book Call for sponsors Sign up to the newsletter \" , \"segment\": \"In hospital, Advanced Life Support (ALS) protocols should be followed. Those who are at risk of anaphylaxis should be supplied with an EpiPen and counselled, along with their close relatives, responsible adults, or carers, about how and when to use it. In the Americas where the Africanised bee has become endemic, any individual who has had more than 50 stings (‘massive stinging’) should be observed for anaphylaxis and toxic envenomation. Venom immunotherapy desensitisation Gradually increasing doses of insect venom are injected subcutaneously to induce immunological tolerance every few weeks for 3–5 years; continue lifelong in clonal mast cell disorders with history of severe reaction or very severe anaphylaxis reactions. Protection usually lasts 1–3 years after discontinuation. Insect venoms are commercially available for honey bee, </cover>","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_05_1004088852#12_1823913740","rank":7,"relevance":3}]},"time":6},"model_calls":53,"document_judgments":31,"relevance_3":8,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
