{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-40863","model_call":69,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"sting reactions\" \"quality of life\" (+ bee wasp hymenoptera venom))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"sting reactions\" \"quality of life\" (+ bee wasp hymenoptera venom))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_05_1004088852#13_1823916429","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1101","rank":1,"snippet":"for systemic reactions Venom immunotherapy desensitisation What is the outcome for bee and wasp stings? Related information Bibliography On DermNet NZ Other websites Skin cancer Dermatology Made Easy Book Call for sponsors Sign up to the newsletter \" , \"segment\": \"Protection usually lasts 1–3 years after discontinuation. Insect venoms are commercially available for honey bee, paper wasp, and yellow jacket European wasp. A Cochrane review reported venom immunotherapy significantly reduces the risk of systemic and large local reactions, and is likely to reduce the risk of anaphylaxis, improving <cover>quality of life. Venom immunotherapy can be recommended for: Children with systemic sting reactions </cover>not limited to the skin Adults with systemic reactions including those limited to the skin High risk factors including beekeepers, patients with clonal mast cell disorders Impaired quality of life following sting reactions NOT recommended for toxic reactions or those sensitised but with no clinical symptoms. What is the outcome for bee and wasp stings? The majority of bee and wasp sting reactions are localised and resolve in hours to days. Episodes are usually rare and simple preventative measures are all that is required. However, sensitisation can develop with repeat episodes, such as ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_05_1004088852#13_1823916429","rank":1,"relevance":3}]},"time":45},"model_calls":69,"document_judgments":37,"relevance_3":8,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
