{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-40863","model_call":69,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1101"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1101"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_05_1004088852#12_1823913740","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1101","rank":2,"snippet":"stinging’) should be observed for anaphylaxis and toxic envenomation. Venom immunotherapy desensitisation Gradually increasing doses of insect venom are injected subcutaneously to induce immunological tolerance every few weeks for 3–5 years; continue lifelong in clonal mast cell disorders with history of severe reaction or very severe anaphylaxis reactions. Protection usually lasts 1–3 years after discontinuation. Insect venoms are commercially available for honey bee, paper wasp, and yellow jacket European wasp. A Cochrane review reported venom immunotherapy significantly reduces the risk of systemic and large local reactions, and is likely to reduce the risk of anaphylaxis, improving <cover>quality of life. Venom immunotherapy can be recommended for: Children with systemic sting reactions </cover>not limited to the skin Adults with systemic reactions including those limited to the skin High risk factors including beekeepers, patients with clonal mast cell disorders Impaired quality of life following sting reactions NOT recommended for toxic reactions or those sensitised but with no clinical symptoms.\" , \"start_char\": 6550.000000 , \"title\": \"Bee and wasp stings | DermNet NZ\" , \"url\": \"http://www.dermnetnz.org/arthropods/bee-and-wasp-stings.html\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_05_1004088852#12_1823913740","rank":2,"relevance":3}]},"time":7},"model_calls":69,"document_judgments":37,"relevance_3":8,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
