{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-40863","model_call":85,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"bee sting allergy\" (+ \"daily life\" lifestyle \"quality of life\" anxiety fear work))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"bee sting allergy\" (+ \"daily life\" lifestyle \"quality of life\" anxiety fear work))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_56_1391197464#9_2762268863","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1113","rank":1,"snippet":"sting? A Word From Verywell \" , \"segment\": \"Take over-the-counter anti-inflammatory drugs ( Advil, Aleve ). Under Your Skin In the unlikely case that part or all of a stinger has become lodged underneath the skin, it will probably work its way out over a few days much like a splinter. If the swelling doesn't go down after a couple of days, you may need to see a doctor to check for infection. How to Treat <cover>Bee Sting Allergy Home Remedies A lot of home remedies may help ease the pain, itching, and swelling of a bee sting. Many of these aren't backed by science but some people claim they work. </cover>Apple cider vinegar: Some people claim that it neutralizes venom and helps reduce swelling. You can soak the sting in diluted vinegar or cover it with a vinegar-soaked cloth for 15 minutes. Honey: It may seem ironic, but this bee product is shown to improve wound healing, reduce inflammation, and kill germs that could cause an infection.\" , \"start_char\": 5180.000000 , \"title\": \"How to Remove a Bee Stinger and "},"time":160},"model_calls":85,"document_judgments":42,"relevance_3":9,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
