{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-40863","model_call":87,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1115"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1115"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_25_1463836094#9_2825253470","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1115","rank":4,"snippet":"char\": 6016.000000 , \"headings\": \"Insect Bites and Stings Insect Bites and Stings This article is for Medical Professionals Hymenoptera stings Systemic reaction to wasp or bee stings Investigation of patients with bee or wasp sting allergy Treatment of patients with bee or wasp sting <cover>allergy Management of bee or wasp stings Blood-sucking flies True bugs (Hemiptera) Ticks (Ixodoidea) Management Harvest mites (Trombiculidae) Join our weekly wellness digest from the best health experts in the business \" , \"segment\": \"Venom immunotherapy (VIT) is recommended for all patients with a severe systemic reaction after a sting. It reduces the chances of a serious allergic reaction to an insect sting and improves quality of life. VIT is the only specific treatment that is currently available for patients with a history of systemic reaction to a Hymenoptera </cover>insect sting. VIT is effective in 95% of patients allergic to wasp venom and about 80% of those allergic to bee venom. VIT is not often recommended for children. A Cochrane review found that approximately 1 in 10 people treated had an allergic reaction during their treatment. The usual duration of VIT is ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_25_1463836094#9_2825253470","rank":4,"relevance":3}]},"time":6},"model_calls":87,"document_judgments":43,"relevance_3":9,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"request","topic_id":"2024-40863","model_call":87,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1115"},"model_calls":87,"document_judgments":43,"relevance_3":9,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-40863","model_call":87,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1115"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1115"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_03_1067819329#3_1802446773","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1115","rank":5,"snippet":"patients with stable, well-controlled asthma. B 20 The efficacy of allergen immunotherapy has been known since 1911, when Noon injected an extract of grass pollen into a person in England whose allergic symptoms coincided with the pollination of grass. 2 Since then, controlled studies have shown that allergen immunotherapy is effective in patients with allergic rhinitis, allergic conjunctivitis, allergic asthma, and allergic reactions to <cover>Hymenoptera venom. 3 – 6 Patients with one or more of these diagnoses are considered for immunotherapy if they have well-defined, clinically relevant allergic triggers that markedly affect their quality of life or daily function, and if they do not attain adequate symptom relief with avoidance measures and pharmacotherapy. Despite proven efficacy, the exact mechanism of allergen immunotherapy remains unknown. Selection of Patients To make a definitive diagnosis of allergy, </cover>IgE-mediated, type I, immediate-hypersensitivity skin testing typically is performed by scratching diluted allergen into the skin surface or by injecting it intradermally. A positive skin test reaction reflects the presence of specific IgE antibodies to the tested allergen, and a correlation of the specific IgE antibodies with the patient’s symptoms, suspected triggers, and allergen exposure is definitive.\" , \"start_char","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_03_1067819329#3_1802446773","rank":5,"relevance":2}]},"time":7},"model_calls":87,"document_judgments":43,"relevance_3":9,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"request","topic_id":"2024-40863","model_call":87,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1115"},"model_calls":87,"document_judgments":43,"relevance_3":9,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-40863","model_call":87,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1115"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1115"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_25_1463836094#8_2825251705","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1115","rank":6,"snippet":"health experts in the business \" , \"segment\": \"Skin tests are also more often positive than serum-specific IgE and correlate better with history. Baseline tryptase should be measured. Those with raised levels have a higher risk of severe systemic reactions. Treatment of patients with bee or wasp sting <cover>allergy All patients with a history of systemic reaction should be immediately provided with a written emergency management plan, an adrenaline (epinephrine) auto-injector and education in its use. With children, appropriate liaison with the school is recommended. Venom immunotherapy (VIT) is recommended for all patients with a severe systemic reaction after a sting. It reduces the chances of a serious allergic reaction to an insect sting and improves quality of life. VIT is the only specific treatment that is currently available for patients with a history of systemic reaction to a Hymenoptera </cover>insect sting. VIT is effective in 95% of patients allergic to wasp venom and about 80% of those allergic to bee venom. VIT is not often recommended for children.\" , \"start_char\": 4702.000000 , \"title\": \"Insect Bites and Stings Treatment Information | Patient\"","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_25_1463836094#8_2825251705","rank":6,"relevance":3}]},"time":6},"model_calls":87,"document_judgments":43,"relevance_3":9,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
